=== FINDINGS (observations, not criterion failures) ===
F1 (test quality, criterion d): the `<no value>` / `&lt;no value&gt;` guard in
   TestSpendingOptimizerRenderSearchPreferences cannot fire for these keys. A
   throwaway probe (rendered the partial with MaxShortfallPctText OMITTED from
   the map) produced
     id="spending-shortfall" ... step="0.1" value=""
   and reported `contains <no value>: false ; contains escaped: false` --
   html/template renders a missing map key as empty inside an attribute, never
   the "<no value>" marker. The criterion's OBSERVABLE (no such marker on any
   rendered surface) is met, live and in unit tests; the guard simply adds no
   protection. The value= assertions carry all the weight and they mutation-kill
   (mutations 6-10). Probe deleted.
F2 (process): the implementation tree was edited mid-verification (dead helper
   CloneSpendingSearchPreferences removed from internal/models/spending_optimizer.go
   between my two `cp -a` snapshots). Re-verified on the later state; no
   criterion is affected. Flagging only because a live-edited tree can silently
   invalidate a checker's baseline.
F3 (domain, not a defect): the live strict-zero path could not be exercised
   end to end -- POST optimize with max_shortfall_pct=0 on demo data returns
   "No spending option met your minimum" with no recommendation token, and the
   subsequent apply correctly returns HTTP 409. Criterion (b)'s strict-zero
   behaviour is covered by the handler and formData unit tests instead, both of
   which mutation-kill.
F4 (cosmetic, pre-existing parser latitude): parseSpendingRequest accepts any
   shortfall in [0,100), so a non-browser POST of e.g. 12.34 would now be
   restored as value="12.34" into an input declaring step="0.1", making the
   restored form client-side invalid until edited. Unreachable through the UI
   (HTML5 validation blocks the submit that would save it). No change requested.
F5 (dual-formatter check, clean): web/static/js/whatif-spending-optimizer.js
   touches none of these five inputs -- its only reference to the search range
   (line 185) formats the SERVER's normalized echo into a text summary and never
   writes an input value. One Go formatter per site (FormatFloat 'f',-1 for the
   percent; template printf "%.2f" for the three money fields); no JS second
   formatter exists to disagree with it.
